\begin_layout Title
Fake Stern-Gerlach Experiment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us now examine the Stern-Gerlach experiment;
 however,
 for our purposes,
 we do not need to incorporate the full physics behind the actual experiment involving particle spin.
 Instead,
 we will follow David Alberto’s (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "McCullochQuantumMechanics101"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) approach and consider two "arbitrary" properties,
 while introducing some mathematics.
 For those interested in an engaging interactive simulation,
 Professor McCulloch’s 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Albert1992"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 materials are worth checking out.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here's an unsettling story about something that can happen to electrons
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
As the reader shall see,
 the identities of the particles will turn out to be completely irrelevant to our concerns here.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The story is true.
 The experiments I will describe have all actually been performed.
 The story concerns two particular physical properties of electrons which it happens to be possible to measure with very great accuracy.
 The precise physical definitions of those two properties don't matter.
 Let's call one of them the "color" of the electron,
 and let's call the other one its "hardness." 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us identify these observables by the following operators:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
0 & 1\\
1 & 0
\end{array}\right),\quad H=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0\\
0 & -1
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It happens to be an empirical fact that the color property of electrons can assume one of only two possible values.
 Every electron which has thus far been encountered in the world has been either a black electron or a white electron.
 None have ever been found to be blue or green.
 Let us associate the eigenvalues 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 with the colors black and white,
 respectively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The same goes for hardness.
 All electrons are either soft ones or hard ones.
 No one has ever seen an electron whose hardness value was anything other than one of those two.
 And analogously,
 we will associate soft and hard electrons with the eigenvalues 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

,
 respectively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the following eigenvectors associated with the eigenvalue of each operator:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|C_{+1}\right\rangle =\left(\begin{array}{c}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\\
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\end{array}\right),\left|C_{-1}\right\rangle =\left(\begin{array}{c}
\frac{-1}{\sqrt{2}}\\
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\end{array}\right),\left|H_{+1}\right\rangle =\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0
\end{array}\right),\left|H_{-1}\right\rangle =\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
1
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $C\left|C_{\pm1}\right\rangle =\pm1\left|C_{\pm1}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $H\left|H_{\pm1}\right\rangle =\pm1\left|H_{\pm1}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
 We can then perform the spectral decomposition of the vectors:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
C & =\left|C_{+1}\right\rangle \left\langle C_{+1}\right|-\left|C_{-1}\right\rangle \left\langle C_{-1}\right|\\
H & =\left|H_{+1}\right\rangle \left\langle H_{+1}\right|-\left|H_{-1}\right\rangle \left\langle H_{-1}\right|
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can write the eigenvectors of an operator in terms of the others:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left|C_{+1}\right\rangle  & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\left|H_{+1}\right\rangle +\left|H_{-1}\right\rangle \right)\\
\left|C_{-1}\right\rangle  & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\left|H_{+1}\right\rangle -\left|H_{-1}\right\rangle \right)\\
\left|H_{+1}\right\rangle  & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\left|C_{+1}\right\rangle -\left|C_{-1}\right\rangle \right)\\
\left|H_{-1}\right\rangle  & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\left|C_{+1}\right\rangle +\left|C_{-1}\right\rangle \right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It's possible to build something called a "color box," which is a device for measuring the color of an electron.
 It's possible to build "hardness boxes" too,
 and they work in just the same way.
 That is,
 if the electron's state vector is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|e\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 Thus,
 the probability of the electron being measured as black (
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

) or white (
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

) is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\left\langle C_{\pm1}|e\right\rangle \right|^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The same applies to hardness 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\left\langle H_{\pm1}|e\right\rangle \right|^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Experiment 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Measurements with hardness and color boxes are repeatable,
 which is something we've grown accustomed to requiring,
 by definition,
 of a "good" measurement of a bona fide physical variable.
 If,
 say,
 a certain electron is measured with a color box to be black,
 and if that electron (without having been tampered with in the meantime) is subsequently fed into another color box,
 then that electron will with certainty emerge from that second color box measured as black again.
 Mathematically,
 this means that after the first measurement,
 the electron's state is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|e\right\rangle =\left|C_{+1}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

;
 consequently,
 at the second color box,
 the probability of it being measured as black again is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P=\left|\left\langle e|C_{+1}\right\rangle \right|^{2}=\left|\left\langle C_{+1}|C_{+1}\right\rangle \right|^{2}=1
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To simplify,
 let's change the notation of the eigenvectors by renaming the letters to indicate color and hardness,
 rather than the eigenvalues:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|C_{+1}\right\rangle =\left|c_{b}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $\left|C_{-1}\right\rangle =\left|c_{w}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $\left|H_{+1}\right\rangle =\left|h_{s}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|C_{+1}\right\rangle =\left|h_{h}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Experiment 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now,
 suppose that it occurs to us to be curious about the possibility that the color and hardness properties of electrons might somehow be related to one another.
 One way to look for such a relation might be to check for correlations between the 
\series bold
values
\series default
 of the hardness and color properties of electrons.
 It's easy to check for correlations like that with our boxes;
 and it turns out (once the checking is done) that no such correlations exist.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Of any large collection of,
 say,
 white electrons,
 all of which are fed into the hardness box,
 precisely half is measured as hard and half as soft.
 The same goes for black electrons fed into the hardness box,
 and the same for hard or soft ones fed into the color boxes.
 The color (hardness) of an electron apparently entails nothing whatever about its hardness (color).
 We can see this explicitly.
 If we are going to measure the hardness of a black electron:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|e\right\rangle =\left|c_{b}\right\rangle =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\left|h_{s}\right\rangle +\left|h_{h}\right\rangle \right)
\]

\end_inset

the probability of each electron being measured as soft is therefore:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
P_{s}\left(\left\langle h_{s}|e\right\rangle \right) & =\left|\left\langle h_{s}\right|\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\left|h_{s}\right\rangle +\left|h_{h}\right\rangle \right)\right)\right|^{2}\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}\left|\left\langle h_{s}|h_{s}\right\rangle +\left\langle h_{s}|h_{h}\right\rangle \right|^{2}\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And the same goes for hard 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{h}\left(\left\langle h_{h}|e\right\rangle \right)=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Experiment 3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose we set up a sequence of three boxes.
 First a color box,
 say,
 then a hardness box,
 and then another color box.
 Consider an electron which emerges through the white aperture of the first color box,
 and thereafter (without having been tampered with) is fed into the hardness box,
 and which happens to emerge from that box through the soft aperture (as half of such electrons will),
 and thereafter (once again with no tampering) is fed into the left aperture of the second color box.
 That electron,
 as it enters that third box,
 is presumably known to be both white and soft.
 As there has been no tampering between boxes here,
 we should expect that the electron will emerge from this third box through the white aperture,
 confirming the result of the first measurement.
 As a matter of fact,
 that isn't what happens.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Precisely half of such electrons emerge from the white aperture of that third box,
 and the other half (the other half,
 that is,
 of those electrons which have been measured to be white and soft by the previous two boxes) emerge from the black aperture.
 Let's turn to the math again.
 From the first box we obtained a white electron 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|c_{w},1\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The same goes for any other pair of results in the first two boxes,
 and the same goes if the
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
color boxes in the above example are replaced with hardness boxes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and the hardness box with a color one.
 Apparently (in the example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
we considered) the presence of the hardness box between the two
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
color boxes itselfconstitutes some sort of color tampering.
 Indeed,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
that hardness box must be what's to blame for changing half of
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
those white electrons to black ones,
 since we already know that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
two color measurements,
 without tampering between the boxes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and without an intervening hardness measurement,
 will invariably
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
produce identical results
\end_layout

