\begin_layout Plain Layout
This experiment is said to illustrate the essential mystery of quantum mechanics
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Another property of quantum systems,
 known as ‘entanglement’,
 sometimes vies for this honour,
 but entanglement relies on this ‘essential mystery’ we are considering here.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 This mystery is embodied in the apparent ability of a system to exhibit properties which,
 from a classical physics point-of-view,
 are mutually contradictory.
 We have already touched on one such instance,
 in which the same physical system can exhibit under different circumstances,
 either particle or wave-like properties,
 otherwise known as wave-particle duality.
 This property of physical systems,
 otherwise known as ‘the superposition of states’,
 must be mirrored in a mathematical language in terms of which the behaviour of such systems can be described and in some sense ‘understood’.
 As we shall see ,
 the two slit experiment is a means by which we arrive at this new mathematical language.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The experiment will be considered in three forms:
 performed with macroscopic particles,
 with waves,
 and with electrons.
 The first two experiments merely show what we expect to see based on our everyday experience.
 It is the third which displays the counterintuitive behaviour of microscopic systems – a peculiar combination of particle and wave like behaviour which cannot be understood in terms of the concepts of classical physics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
An Experiment with Bullets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Imagine an experimental setup in which a machine gun is spraying bullets at a screen in which there are two narrow openings,
 or slits which may or may not be covered.
 Bullets that pass through the openings will then strike a further screen,
 the detection or observation screen,
 behind the first,
 and the point of impact of the bullets on this screen are noted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Suppose,
 in the first instance,
 that this experiment is carried out with only one slit opened,
 slit 1 say.
 A first point to note is that the bullets arrive in ‘lumps’,
 (assuming indestructible bullets),
 i.e.
 every bullet that leaves the gun (and makes it through the slits) arrives as a whole somewhere on the detection screen.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Not surprisingly,
 what would be observed is the tendency for the bullets to strike the screen in a region somewhere immediately opposite the position of the open slit,
 but because the machine gun is firing erratically,
 we would expect that not all the bullets would strike the screen in exactly the same spot,
 but to strike the screen at random,
 though always in a region roughly opposite the opened slit.
 We can represent this experimental outcome by a curve 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{1}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 which is simply such that 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{1}\left(x\right)\delta x$
\end_inset

 is the probability of a bullet landing in the range 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x,x+\delta x\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
If we were to cover this slit and open the other,
 then what we would observe is the tendency for the bullets to strike the screen opposite this opened slit,
 producing a curve 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{2}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 similar to 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{1}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Finally,
 suppose that both slits are opened.
 We would then observe the bullets would sometimes come through slit 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 and sometimes through slit 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

 – varying between the two possibilities in a random way – producing two piles behind each slit in a way that is simply the sum of the results that would be observed with one or the other slit opened,
 i.e 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{12}\left(x\right)=P_{1}\left(x\right)+P_{2}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In order to quantify this last statement,
 we construct a histogram with which to specify the way the bullets spread themselves across the observation screen.
 We start by assuming that this screen is divided up into boxes of width 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta x$
\end_inset

,
 and then count the number of bullets that land in each box.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Suppose that the number of bullets fired from the gun is 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is a large number.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta N\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 bullets land in the box occupying the range 
\begin_inset Formula $x+\delta x$
\end_inset

 then we can plot a histogram of 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta N/(N\delta x)$
\end_inset

,
 the fraction of all the bullets that arrive,
 per unit length,
 in each interval over the entire width of the screen.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
If the number of bullets is very large,
 and the width 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta x$
\end_inset

 sufficiently small,
 then the histogram will define a smooth curve,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

say.
 What this quantity 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 represents can be gained by considering
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P\left(x\right)\delta x=\frac{\delta N}{N}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
which is the fraction of all the bullets fired from the gun that end up landing on the screen in region 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $x+\delta x$
\end_inset

.
 In other words,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is very large,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x\right)\delta x$
\end_inset

 approximates to the probability that any given bullet will arrive at the detection screen in the range 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $x+\delta x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We can do the same in the two cases in which one or the other of the two slits are open.
 Thus,
 if slit 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is open,
 then we get the curve 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{i}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

.
 What we are then saying is that if we leave both slits open,
 then the result will be just the sum of the two single slit curves,
 i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{12}\left(x\right)=P_{1}\left(x\right)+P_{2}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In other words,
 the probability of a bullet striking the screen in some region 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $x+\delta$
\end_inset

 when both slits are opened is just the sum of the probabilities of the bullet landing in region when one slit and then the other is closed.
 This is all perfectly consistent with what we understand about the properties and behaviour of macroscopic objects —
 they arrive in indestructible lumps,
 and the probability observed with two slits open is just the sum of the probabilities with each open individually
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
An Experiment with Waves
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Now repeat the experiment with waves.
 For definiteness,
 let us suppose that the waves are light waves of wavelength 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

.
 The waves pass through the slits and then impinge on the screen where we measure the intensity of the waves as a function of position along the screen.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
First perform this experiment with one of the slits open,
 the other closed.
 The resultant intensity distribution is then a curve which peaks behind the position of the open slit,
 much like the curve obtained in the experiment using bullets.
 Call it 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{1}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

,
 which we know is just the square of the amplitude of the wave incident at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 which originated from slit 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

.
 If we deal only with the electric field,
 and let the amplitude
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The word ‘amplitude’ is used here to represent the value of the wave at some point in time and space,
 and is not used to represent the maximum value of an oscillating wave.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of the wave at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 be 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(x,t\right)=E\left(x\right)e^{-i\omega t}$
\end_inset

 in complex notation,
 then the intensity of the wave at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 will be
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
I_{1}\left(x\right)=\left|E_{1}\left(x,t\right)\right|^{2}=E_{1}\left(x\right)^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
lose this slit and open the other.
 Again we get a curve which peaks behind the position of the open slit.
 Call it 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{2}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Now open both slits.
 What results is a curve on the screen 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{12}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 which oscillates between maxima and minima – an interference pattern.
 In fact,
 the theory of interference of waves tells us that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
I_{12}\left(x\right) & =\left|E_{1}\left(x,t\right)+E_{2}\left(x,t\right)\right|^{2}\\
 & =\left|E_{1}\left(x\right)e^{-i\omega t}+E_{2}\left(x\right)e^{-i\omega t}\right|^{2}\\
 & =\left(E_{1}\left(x\right)e^{-i\omega t}+E_{2}\left(x\right)e^{-i\omega t}\right)\left(E_{1}\left(x\right)e^{i\omega t}+E_{2}\left(x\right)e^{i\omega t}\right)\\
 & =\left(E_{1}\left(x\right)^{2}+E_{2}\left(x\right)^{2}+\right)\left(E_{1}\left(x\right)e^{i\omega t}+E_{2}\left(x\right)e^{i\omega t}\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Como pode ser 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{12}\left(x\right)=E_{1}\left(x\right)^{2}+E_{2}\left(x\right)^{2}+2E_{1}\left(x\right)E\left(x\right)\cos\left(2\pi d\sin\theta/\lambda\right)$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

